<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pitti Palace</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pitti Palace is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arno">Arno</a>, begun in the 1450s for the banker Luca Pitti and bought in 1550 by Eleonora of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-i-de-medici">Cosimo I de' Medici</a>. Enlarged over the following centuries, it became the main residence of the grand dukes of Tuscany and later of the Savoy kings during Florence's time as Italian capital. It now houses several museums.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pitti-palace.jpg" alt="Pitti Palace" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Almaak · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palazzo_Pitti_nel_tardo_pomeriggio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
